through this monitor all the  time we won't be able to speak naturally  and i certainly agree  that the rules are just there to kind of  make us more attentive but if we try to  remember the language via rules if we  try to think through the rules each time  we want to speak we won't get very far  uh and as i've said before i'm quite  happy to  have things in a text remain uncertain  to me i won't go and search the grammar  rule i'll just  soldier on so i think his monitor theory  as in other words as i understand it in  other words don't become too reliant on  grammar or you'll always have to go to  this monitor every time you want to  say something or understand something  he talks about effective filters like  feelings that we have that prevent us  from learning and that is certainly true  and and i've talked about this as a  major issue for adults who are afraid to  sound childish who are  otherwise inhibited and prevented from  just communicating and taking the  language in the way children do  so i would agree with them there  he talks about and others do as well  there's reference to vesatsuki i think  his name is  uh famous uh russian uh  writer on education this whole idea that  we want that proximate level that  i plus one the to the extent that we are  in just that next level of difficulty uh  we are going to do well in our learning  uh i think even the whole flow theory  apply you know refers to that and i  won't even try to pronounce the name of  this  nihilist whatever hungarian writer who  wrote about  flow in other words if you're in a  situation where whatever you're  attempting to do is just a little  difficult for you and you are able to do  it you have a great sense of  satisfaction and achievement you're in a  powerful motivated learning  mood  the only thing i would say to that is  that to me  if we are input-based language learners  then the selection of what to use as  content to my mind  uh it's more motivating and therefore  better for me  to have content that is interesting  rather than just to have content that's  just a little bit difficult so i have a  very boring text  that's just a little bit difficult but  it's of no interest to me that doesn't  do me much good  and particularly nowadays that we don't  have to rely on a conventional book with  a conventional dictionary we can read on  the internet we can hear the audio  uh we can learn words and phrases retain  them and view them all these other tools  make it possible to take on content that  is that not i plus one but i plus five  uh if we are motivated to learn and to  fight our way through that content so to  that extent i disagree there  uh he also talks about the natural order  that there is a natural order  with which we will learn grammar so that  you can't  learn something until you are ready to  learn it that there is a natural order i  have no idea whether this is true or not  but i do know  that  it is not because a teacher teaches  something or because you deliberately  study something that you're going to  learn it  when  a grammar pattern clicks in and starts  to feel natural is not something that we  control  and that is another reason why i don't  mind not understanding  not getting certain language patterns  and that's another reason why i think  it's not a good idea to test people  based on grammar patterns that have been  taught the fact that it has been taught  doesn't mean that it's going to be  learned it'll click in at some point in  the future  now if you go to the website the lev  reference to krashen's work  you'll notice that a lot of the  discussion refers to what happens in a  classroom  and since i believe that learning takes  place primarily outside the classroom  that the classroom is there only to  stimulate the learner  to  get them turned on to learning  you know i suddenly began to begin to  realize that that really there's sort of  two kinds of learning there's the  feedlot if we think of cattle  there's the feedlot learning  where a bunch of cattle are impend in a  small area  and the rancher feeds them whatever he  wants to feed them and they stand in one  spot and munch on it more or less  whereas the grazing model is that the  cattle or the sheep or whatever they are  they wander all over the place and they  don't stand in one spot and  and and eat the grass until it's bare  although i've never been a cows i don't  know but they seem to i know the image  at least is that they're wandering  around the hillside munching some there  munching some here then they wander back  to the same spot again and munch some  more and that's kind of the image to me  of learning  we do a little bit here we don't want to  and this is the i think the biggest  problem that inexperienced learners have  is that they want to nail things down  they want to eat that grass right down  to their roots they want to try to  master this lesson master this  vocabulary master this grammar  and it is a futile exercise it's  extremely wasteful of your time and  energy and it leads to frustration  it's better you're better to go and and  like you know i'll often recommend to  beginners buy three beginner books  and go through essentially the same  kind of content from three different  approaches or you know if you're a link  take lots of different beginner content  don't necessarily just stay with one  graze around and cover  the same thing in different ways  uh  and and uh go back over things i mean i  have been at russian for three years i  take great delight in going back to my  beginner books  and trying now to and i'm reminding  myself of things that i've learned and  forgotten even though i'm quite capable  of understanding quite difficult stuff i  know i'm going to make mistakes and very  simple things and so i'm able to go back  so you know i think that that  i'm a grazer i'm out exploring  deciding what i want to munch on  wandering the hillside not worrying  about what i leave behind  i'm not a feedlot learner pending  waiting for  the rancher to give me  my  oats or my hay or whatever he's decided  to feed me  or  antibiotics or steroids or whatever he's  going to feed me  so i think a lot of the discussion about  even some of the things that are on that  thread effective filters things that  happen in a classroom  it's quite different if we're out  grazing  so there you have it it's uh  again further discussion on crash i  recommend that you visit this thread get  into this discussion about stephen crash  and the tremendous influence that he has  had  and that he is now applying to the whole  issue of literacy in the united states  where again he's appealing for a more  natural and a pleasurable approach to  improving literacy rather than the  over-teaching that the established  teaching institutions  uh seem to want to impose over teaching  over-testing which is only going to  discourage people from reading so thank  you for listening
 

